Reducing the extra space further
As a final remark, if we are allowed to modify the grid, it is possible to reduce the extra space all the way down to O(1). For this, we'll want to use DFS, not BFS, but we need to make it iterative.

Note that iterative DFS is not commonly expected in interviews, so it is generally not worth learning it. We just include it for fun
.

Warning: the approach shown here iterates through the entire neighbor list of a node each time we need to find an unvisited neighbor; this is fine in grids because each cell has at most 4 neighbors, but it would change the time complexity in general graphs. Making iterative DFS as efficient as recursive DFS for general graphs is a bit more tricky.

The idea is to encode in the grid itself the position of each node in the "recursion stack":

Since the input already uses values 0 and 1, we can use negative numbers to track the stack (-1 for the first cell in the stack, -2 for the second, and so on).
We also need to keep track of the current head of the stack with two variables, head_r and head_c, which correspond to the r and c parameters usually received by the recursive visit() function.
At each iteration, we look at the neighbors of (head_r, head_c) and, if we find any 1, we go there. If not, we "backtrack" by looking for the neighbor which is the previous element in the stack, and mark the current cell with 2 to indicate it's already visited.
Here is the implementation:

class CountIslandsInPlace {
  private int[][] grid;
  private int[][] directions = { { -1, 0 }, { 1, 0 }, { 0, 1 }, { 0, -1 } };
  private int R, C;

  private boolean inBounds(int r, int c) {
    return 0 <= r && r < R && 0 <= c && c < C;
  }

  private int[] findParent(int r, int c) {
    int currentValue = grid[r][c];
    for (int[] dir : directions) {
      int nbrR = r + dir[0];
      int nbrC = c + dir[1];
      if (inBounds(nbrR, nbrC) && grid[nbrR][nbrC] == currentValue + 1) {
        return new int[] { nbrR, nbrC };
      }
    }
    return null;
  }

  private int[] findUnvisitedNeighbor(int r, int c) {
    for (int[] dir : directions) {
      int nbrR = r + dir[0];
      int nbrC = c + dir[1];
      if (inBounds(nbrR, nbrC) && grid[nbrR][nbrC] == 1) {
        return new int[] { nbrR, nbrC };
      }
    }
    return null;
  }

  private void iterativeDfs(int startR, int startC) {
    int stackLevel = -1;
    grid[startR][startC] = stackLevel;
    int headR = startR;
    int headC = startC;
    while (stackLevel < 0) {
      int[] nbr = findUnvisitedNeighbor(headR, headC);
      if (nbr != null) {
        stackLevel--;
        grid[nbr[0]][nbr[1]] = stackLevel;
        headR = nbr[0];
        headC = nbr[1];
      } else {
        // No unvisited neighbors, backtrack
        stackLevel++;
        int[] parent = findParent(headR, headC);
        if (parent != null) {
          headR = parent[0];
          headC = parent[1];
        }
      }
    }
  }

  public int solve(int[][] grid) {
    this.grid = grid;
    R = grid.length;
    C = grid[0].length;

    int islandCount = 0;
    for (int startR = 0; startR < R; startR++) {
      for (int startC = 0; startC < C; startC++) {
        if (grid[startR][startC] == 1) {
          iterativeDfs(startR, startC);
          islandCount++;
        }
      }
    }
    return islandCount;
  }
}

